' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---

' The automated pass reads data members and little else, so in this component
' it draws the process shell as empty boxes: application, host and parser hold
' only methods, the two exception types lose their what(), and the whole
' message-registration facet is invisible because the per-entity registrars
' are free functions. The blocks below fill those boxes and draw the
' relationships the pass cannot see.
'
' Fully-qualified class names are used rather than a reopened namespace block,
' because this PlantUML version crashes when a namespace that already holds
' classes is declared a second time; redeclaring a class merges its members
' into the box the automated pass drew.

class ores::iam::service::app::application {
    +application()
    +run(io_ctx : io_context&, cfg : config::options) : awaitable<void>
    {static} -make_context(db_opts : database_options) : ores::database::context
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::app::application"
The composition root and the service's start-up order:
connect the NATS client, stand up the Postgres event pipeline
(an event_bus and a postgres_event_source over their own
database context), subscribe the ten entity mappings through
the event registrar and start the source, then hand over to
the shared signing-service lifecycle. That lifecycle builds
the RS256 signer from cfg.jwt_private_key, lets
ores.iam.core's registrar subscribe every IAM subject,
co_spawns the heartbeat publisher, waits on
SIGINT/SIGTERM/SIGQUIT, and drains NATS. The event source is
stopped last.
end note
note bottom of "ores::iam::service::app::application"
run() is const and keeps no state: the connection, the two
database contexts, the event pipeline and the subscriptions
are all locals, so the service's lifetime is the coroutine's.
make_context asks ores.database's context_factory for a pool
of db_opts.pool_size connections, ten attempts one second
apart, and stamps the context's service account as
db_opts.user. The context is built twice, once for the event
source and once for the handler registrar, because those are
two independent consumers of the database.
end note

class ores::iam::service::app::host {
    {static} +execute(args : vector<string>, std_output : ostream&, error_output : ostream&, io_ctx : io_context&) : awaitable<int>
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::app::host"
The console workflow, and its return value is the exit code.
Every member is a static method, which is why the automated
pass drew this box empty. It delegates to ores.service's
run_host_async<parser, application>, which parses the command
line, returns EXIT_SUCCESS on an empty parse result (the
--help and --version paths), starts logging from the parsed
options, logs the argument vector at info and the
configuration at debug, default-constructs the application
and awaits run(). A failure is logged and rethrown, and
main() maps it to the exit code: a parser_exception is a
plain EXIT_FAILURE, a db_connection_exception is
exit_code::db_connection_failed, and anything else is
reported on the error stream.
end note

class ores::iam::service::config::parser {
    +parse(arguments : vector<string>, info : ostream&, error : ostream&) : optional<options>
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::config::parser"
An empty optional is the help/version answer, not an error:
the help text, the version, the GPL notice and the build
details are written to the info stream and the caller exits
zero. A parse failure writes "Usage error: ..." and the
"Try '--help'" line to the error stream and then throws
parser_exception, which main() turns into EXIT_FAILURE
without printing anything further. Logging is unavailable
here by design, because the logger starts only after the
parse succeeds, so the error stream is the only diagnostic.
The option set is the shared service set (common, logging,
database, NATS) plus IAM's own --jwt-private-key, read from
ORES_IAM_SERVICE_JWT_PRIVATE_KEY when the option is absent
and defaulting to the empty string.
end note

class ores::iam::service::app::application_exception {
    +application_exception(message : string_view = "")
    +what() : const char*
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::app::application_exception"
The declared fatal-error type for the running application.
It derives from boost::exception as well as std::exception,
so the shared host runner logs diagnostic_information() for
it rather than what(); no source in the component throws it
today.
end note

class ores::iam::service::config::parser_exception {
    +parser_exception(message : string_view = "")
    +what() : const char*
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::config::parser_exception"
Thrown by parser::parse for a program_options failure and for
a nested parser_exception, in both cases after the usage line
has already reached the error stream.
end note

note bottom of "ores::iam::service::config::options"
operator<< is what the debug-level "Configuration:" line of
the shared host runner streams, and it writes the whole
configuration as JSON with one field replaced:
jwt_private_key becomes "***". That key signs every token this
service issues, so a log reader who saw it could mint a
session for any account.
end note

' The message-registration facet. The automated pass draws the composer empty
' (its only member is a static method) and the ten per-entity registrars not
' at all, because they are free functions.

class ores::iam::messaging::registrar {
    {static} +register_handlers(nats : ores::nats::service::client&, ctx : ores::database::context, signer : jwt_authenticator, service_password : string) : vector<subscription>
}
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::registrar"
ores.iam.core's registrar, not this component's: it is drawn
because application::run drives it. It wires every IAM subject
on the ores.iam.service queue group: signup, login, logout,
public key, refresh and service login; bootstrap status,
initial admin and tenant provisioning; accounts, account
parties and account operations; sessions and their operations;
roles and authorization; tenants, tenant statuses and tenant
types; system reset; the publish-from-DQ steps; the
ores.variability.system_setting_changed subscription that
reloads token settings without a restart; and the generic
history.v1.get subject. It logs its subscription count, then
arms the party cache with a token minted from IAM's own
service account and the password handed in here.
end note

class ores::iam::service::messaging::event_registrar {
    {static} +register_event_mappings(event_source : postgres_event_source&, event_bus : event_bus&, nats : nats::client&) : vector<subscription>
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::messaging::event_registrar"
The one hand-maintained line-per-entity call site, the same
shape ores.refdata, ores.dq and ores.synthetic use. It calls
the ten generated per-entity registrars below and takes
ownership of the subscriptions they return, because a mapping
with no live subscription never reaches its subject.
application::run is its only caller in the tree.
end note

' The ten per-entity event registrars, one per IAM table that publishes a
' change event. Each is a free function, so the automated pass cannot see it.

class ores::iam::service::messaging::register_account_event_mapping {
    {static} +register_account_event_mapping(event_source : postgres_event_source&, event_bus : event_bus&, nats : nats::client&) : subscription
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::messaging::register_account_event_mapping"
Free function, one per entity, and the reason the box is
hand-drawn. Each does two things:
register_entity_event_mapping binds the table's own Postgres
NOTIFY channel to the entity's event type, and event_bus's
subscribe returns the subscription that republishes each
notification to NATS. One payload is addressed by three
subjects, so the event's action completes the subject:
prefix.created, prefix.updated or prefix.deleted. The other
nine carry the same three arguments and the same two steps.
end note
note bottom of "ores::iam::service::messaging::register_account_event_mapping"
The ten channel-to-prefix pairs, one per IAM table:
ores_iam_accounts to iam.v1.accounts_events,
ores_iam_account_contact_informations to
iam.v1.account_contact_informations_events,
ores_iam_account_types to iam.v1.account_types_events,
ores_iam_login_info to iam.v1.login_info_events,
ores_iam_permissions to iam.v1.permissions_events,
ores_iam_roles to iam.v1.roles_events,
ores_iam_sessions to iam.v1.sessions_events,
ores_iam_tenants to iam.v1.tenants_events,
ores_iam_tenant_statuses to iam.v1.tenant_statuses_events
and ores_iam_tenant_types to iam.v1.tenant_types_events.
These are the only entity change mappings this component
registers.
end note

